module display_generic.pos_2

// A GENERIC SUM INSTANCE IN A SLOT: `Option[str]` printed through the shell's
// own `@display`. The oracle prints `Some(hi)` -- asserted by std's own doc
// example at `prelude/protocols.nv:647`.
//
// THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE A RECORD OF MINE WAS WRONG, and the correction is
// the point. Earlier the same day I wrote -- in the plan, in this directory's
// `neg_1` and to the integrator -- that `Option` was held by TWO independent
// blockers: no symbol in the shell AND no spelling in novac. The first half was
// FALSE. I had grepped for the spelling novac WOULD build
// (`NovaOpt_*_method_display`), found nothing, and read that as "the shell does
// not carry it". The shell carries it under the spelling the ORACLE builds:
// `Nova_Option_method_display_nova_str`, defined in `shell.tpl.c` at :6338.
//
// A grep for the name you EXPECT answers a question about your expectation.
// The question was "does the shell carry this method", and the way to ask it is
// by the method, not by the name you would have given it.
//
// So there was ONE blocker -- novac could not spell a generic sum -- and it is
// closed: `c_instance_method` now builds the family form
// `Nova_<Head>_method_<name>_<arg>...`, read off the shell rather than invented.
//
// WHY `Option[str]` AND NOT `Option[int]`, said so the next reader does not
// think the int case is broken: the shell carries only the instances the probe
// called, and `shell_probe.nv` calls `@display` on an `Option[str]`. The int
// one is refused BY NAME, correctly, and that is a question for the probe, not
// for the compiler.

fn pick(f bool) -> Option[str] {
    if f {
        return Some("hi")
    }
    None
}

fn main() {
    ro o = pick(true)
    println("${o}")
}
